From 95dfd4d3c0b85ffd4328799baf8cb7cdff7bae17 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 19:10:12 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(update): review findings on the pre-update backup
 progress [daveey]
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Snapshot (hermes_cli/backup_sqlite.py):
- The clone path is decided before any lock: skipped (with the reason logged
  and in clone_error) on Python < 3.12, where the lock connection's close could
  checkpoint the WAL into state.db on the main thread, and on a filesystem
  where a throwaway probe file cannot be cloned (ext4, NTFS), so those never
  take state.db's write lock just to fail.
- The lock is tried without waiting first; when another writer holds it the
  script says so ({"event":"backup_lock_wait"}) and waits up to 2 s. The result
  carries lock_wait_ms next to lock_ms.
- The Linux clone source descriptor closes in its own finally.
- The light clone check (header, open, schema_version, WAL fold) says plainly
  that a corrupt state.db is backed up as it is on that path, not cancelled.

Desktop:
- The backup stage shows at once (indeterminate "start"), so an older snapshot
  script with no progress lines still shows "Backing up before updating…", and
  the start is always logged.
- The POSIX hand-off leaves "Checking the backup…" for "Backup done. Starting
  the updater…" as soon as the snapshot returns.
- A snapshot that ignores SIGTERM past its deadline is SIGKILLed after 10 s.

Tests: Python < 3.12 path, the probe keeping the lock untaken, a passive
checkpoint between the db and WAL clones, SIGTERM during the clone verify, the
lock-wait test now waits for the child's own lock_wait line instead of a sleep;
SIGKILL escalation, the start stage with a silent script, and restart strictly
after the backup in the hand-off.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
